          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get migraines maybe six times a month.",
          "answer": "Thank you for sharing that. Headaches about six times a month are worth taking seriously. I can't tell you what is causing yours, but I can share what the guidance says.\n\n**How frequency is classified:** Migraine is usually described as episodic when it happens on fewer than 15 days a month. It is called chronic when headache occurs on 15 or more days a month, with at least 8 of those having migraine features, for more than three months. At about six a month, you would fall in the episodic range (*Migraine*, general clinical reference).\n\n**When to talk to a clinician:** Frequent attacks are a good reason to see a clinician, especially if they are getting more common, are hard to manage, or are affecting sleep or daily life. The sleep note in this resource says headaches on waking most mornings, or headaches that wake you at night, should be assessed rather than handled with sleep habits alone (*Sleep and migraine*).\n\n**Pain medicine use:** Taking acute headache medicine too often can keep headaches going. Guidance sets limits on how many days a month these medicines should be used. Check with a clinician or pharmacist about your own use (*Migraine: acute treatment and medication-overuse limits*).\n\n**General lifestyle guidance:** Keep sleep and meal times fairly regular, including on weekends. Sudden changes in sleep or caffeine are the part most often linked to attacks. Caffeine affects both headaches and sleep, so changes to it are best made gradually (*Sleep and migraine*).\n\n**Seek urgent care right away** if a headache is sudden and reaches full intensity within about a minute, or comes with fever and a stiff neck, new weakness, numbness, confusion, or trouble speaking or seeing. Also seek urgent care after any head injury, or with a red, painful eye and halos around lights (*Sudden severe headache and other dangerous headache patterns*).\n\nI was not able to look up detailed management options in this session, so I cannot go further into treatments. Would you like to know more about how migraine is diagnosed, what to track before a clinic visit, or how sleep and caffeine may affect attacks?\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
